From 1060518c2bc9d646b2357c4a991ce3504db61660 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work5 <adbtrr_work5@local>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 05:54:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] examples/crashrpt_test: demonstrate crash diagnostic capture

Exercises /dev/crashrpt end to end, showing how a product application
publishes diagnostic data when the system is about to assert.

Data held
---------

  500 nodes of 48 bytes
   50 nodes of 64 bytes

Each node carries a pointer to a message string of up to 255 bytes.  The
message is not stored inside the node, so on disk it follows its node and
both end up in the report.

The structure sizes are part of the requirement, so they are checked at run
time before anything else happens rather than assumed.  They depend on the
pointer being four bytes; on a target where that does not hold the check
reports the mismatch instead of silently writing a different format.

The data is built once at start-up while the system is healthy.  Allocating
several hundred blocks on the way down is exactly the kind of work that
should not be attempted, and building it during normal operation also
mirrors how a real product accumulates diagnostic state.

Temporary file and rename
-------------------------

The report is written to crashrpt.tmp and renamed to crashrpt_r<n>.bin only
after the write has completed and been flushed with fsync().  A reader never
observes a partial report - the final name either exists and is complete or
does not exist at all.  Any failure removes the temporary file.

Serialisation
-------------

A 32 byte header records the magic, version, reason code, node counts and
node sizes.  Each record is the raw node, a one byte message length, then
the message.  One byte is sufficient because the message is capped at 255.

The message pointer inside each node is zeroed in the copy that is written,
since its value has no meaning outside the running process and leaving it
would make the output unreproducible.

The write path does no allocation.  Everything it needs was set up during
initialisation.

Usage
-----

  crashrpt_test init      build the data and register the reporter
  crashrpt_test status    show state and the largest possible report
  crashrpt_test crash     raise an assert to exercise the path

Disabled by default.  Depends on CONFIG_CRASH_REPORT.
